      "textEn": "36\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nSong—song; Instrument—and instrumental music; Saying—with words; Brahma—of the Vedas; Saying—with sound; More—abundant;\nAbhyayat—went ahead; He—that; Hrishikesham—to Lord Krishna; Pranas—of the senses; Pranam—life or consciousness; Iva—like;\nSimilar; Aahata—venerable. With the high sound of Vedic hymns, songs and musical instruments began to resonate, and the king\nWith great respect, proceeded to meet Lord Hrishikesh, just as the senses move forward to meet the life-force. Meaning: Here, Lord Krishna is described as Hrishikesh, meaning the master of the senses, and by King Yudhishthira,\nto go quickly to God is described as a desireful meeting of the senses with consciousness. The senses are useless without consciousness—the senses function through consciousness. Similarly, when\nindividual souls become devoid of Krishna consciousness, that is, divine love, then they enter into a futile and illusory conflict,\nwhich is called worldly existence or the material world.\nA pure devotee like King Yudhishthira is never without the company of God,\nbecause he always keeps God in his heart. Even then, when they\nsee them after a long separation, they experience special joy,\nas has been described here. Having seen, with hearts troubled, Krishna, with love, the Pandavas. Chiradrishtam priyatam sasvaje yatha punah punah. 25. Meaning of the word\n\nDrishtva—Having seen; Viklinna—Moved; Hrudayah—Their heart; Krishnam—To Lord Krishna; Snehena—With affection; Pandavah—Pandu-\nson; Chirat—For a long time; Drishtam—Seen; Priya-Tamam—To one's very dear friend; Sasvaje—Embraced; Ath—After;\nTatpashcha—Thereafter; Punah—Again; Punah—Again and again; Phir—Again and again. When King Yudhishthira saw his beloved friend Lord Krishna after such a long separation,\nhis heart melted with affection, and he repeatedly embraced the Lord. Having abandoned the two, he went to Rama's abode.\nThe king's body was struck by Mukunda's body, and he was immediately afflicted with misfortune.\nOn the lips of the eyes, the unsteady, restless, and forgetful world. 26. Lexicon"
