्दार्थ",
      "textEn": "0\n\nReceived—having come; Nishamya—having heard; Nar—of men; Lochana—of eyes; Paan— of drinking; Patram— object or store;\nAutsakya— out of curiosity; Vishlathita— loose; Kesha— hair; Dukul— of clothes; Bandhaha— and knots; Sadyaha— immediately;\nVisrijya— having abandoned; Griha— of householders; Karma— work; Patin— to their husbands; Cha— and; Talpe— in bed; Drashtum— for seeing;\nYayuha— having gone; Yuvatayaha— of young women; Smam— without doubt; Nar-Indra— of the king; Marga— on the path; When the young women of the city heard that Lord Krishna, the abode of joy for the eyes of humans,\nhas come, they quickly reached the highway to see him. She gave up her household chores, even leaving her husbands in bed. Out of curiosity, their hair strands and clothes became loose. Tasminsuskulam ibhashwarathadvipadhibhih\nKrishnamasabaryamupalabhya gruhadhirudhaah.\nHaving adorned the women with flowers,\nWelcome with joy, O glorious one, beholding with awe. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nTasmin—on that (path); Su—extremely; Sankule—crowded; Ibh—elephants; Ashva—horses; Ratha—chariots; Dvipadbhih—as well as\nPedestrians; Krishnam—to Krishna; Sa-bharyam—with his wives; Upalabhya—seen; Griha—of houses;\nAdhirudhaah—climbed on the roofs; Naryah—women; Vikiarya—scattered; Kusumaih—with flowers; Manasa—in the minds; Upaguhya—with the lips; Upaghraha—with the arms; Su-swagatam—a warm welcome; Vidadhuha—given to him; Utsmaya—laughing; Viksithena—with the eyes; Chitvanas—with the minds. There was a large crowd of elephants, horses, chariots, and infantry on the highway, so the women\nclimbed onto the roofs of their houses, from where they saw Krishna and his queens. The women of the city\nshowered flowers on God, embraced him in their hearts, and with laughter-filled glances,\nexpressed their heartfelt welcome.\nMeaning: Srila Sridhar Swami's commentary is that women, through their loving expressions,\nasked curious questions about the convenience of God's pilgrimage